<sect1 id="md_docs_2content_2dev_2scatter__field_1autotoc_md42">
<title>2. Design Principles</title>
<sect2 id="md_docs_2content_2dev_2scatter__field_1autotoc_md43">
<title>Uniform Instance Representation</title>
<para>All discrete spatial entities inherit a minimal three-attribute representation:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><computeroutput>position</computeroutput> (<computeroutput>glm::vec3</computeroutput>): 3D spatial coordinate ($x, y$ ground position and elevation $z$).</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><computeroutput>class_id</computeroutput> (<computeroutput>uint32_t</computeroutput>): Integer category identifier mapping to a biological species, rock material, or mesh asset.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><computeroutput>radius</computeroutput> (<computeroutput>float</computeroutput>): Characteristic collision or influence radius.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><computeroutput>Tree</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>Rock</computeroutput> are type aliases of <computeroutput>ScatterItem</computeroutput> (<computeroutput>using Tree = ScatterItem;</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>using Rock = ScatterItem;</computeroutput>).</para>
</sect2>
<sect2 id="md_docs_2content_2dev_2scatter__field_1autotoc_md44">
<title>Inheritance and Reuse</title>
<para><computeroutput>ScatterField</computeroutput> provides generic spatial queries and transforms common to any scattered entity:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>Spatial queries</bold>: Bounding box evaluation, point queries, class-based filtering (<computeroutput>filter_by_class</computeroutput>).</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><bold>Spatial organization</bold>: Delaunay triangulation densification (<computeroutput>densify</computeroutput>), spatial collision pruning (<computeroutput>prune_collisions</computeroutput>).</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><bold>Raster integration</bold>: Elevation snapping (<computeroutput>set_elevation_from_terrain</computeroutput>), rasterization into density maps (<computeroutput>to_density_map</computeroutput>), and stamp-based heightmap rasterization (<computeroutput>to_heightmap</computeroutput>).</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para>Both <computeroutput>Forest</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>RockField</computeroutput> publicly inherit from <computeroutput>ScatterField</computeroutput>. They inherit the full suite of spatial operations without duplicating spatial indexing or container logic.</para>
</sect2>
<sect2 id="md_docs_2content_2dev_2scatter__field_1autotoc_md45">
<title>Descriptor Separation</title>
<para>Instance containers store only per-instance spatial state (<computeroutput>position</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>class_id</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>radius</computeroutput>). Metadata and physical parameters are separated into lightweight descriptor structures:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><computeroutput>Species</computeroutput>: Biological traits (growth limits, relative seeding weight, shade/canopy competition factors).</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><computeroutput>RockDistribution</computeroutput>: Geomorphological parameters (Pareto power-law exponent $\alpha$, minimum/maximum radius, embed ratio).</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para>Simulation algorithms take instance fields alongside descriptor arrays. This keeps memory footprint minimal when storing millions of scattered entities.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>
<sect1 id="md_docs_2content_2dev_2scatter__field_1autotoc_md46">
<title>3. Subsystem Connections</title>
<sect2 id="md_docs_2content_2dev_2scatter__field_1autotoc_md47">
<title>Flora Subsystem (&lt;tt&gt;Forest&lt;/tt&gt;)</title>
<para><computeroutput>Forest</computeroutput> models vegetation and tree distributions.<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>Seeding</bold>: <computeroutput>seed_forest_density</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>seed_forest_species</computeroutput> populate <computeroutput>Forest</computeroutput> instances using environmental suitability rasters (slope, elevation, moisture) and species weights.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><bold>Simulation</bold>: Biological competition is simulated via <computeroutput>grow_forest_competition</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>simulate_forest_cycle</computeroutput>, which iterate through the <computeroutput>Tree</computeroutput> instances and adjust radii or eliminate suppressed individuals based on canopy overlap.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
</sect2>
<sect2 id="md_docs_2content_2dev_2scatter__field_1autotoc_md48">
<title>Rocks Subsystem (&lt;tt&gt;RockField&lt;/tt&gt;)</title>
<para><computeroutput>RockField</computeroutput> models talus slopes, scree fields, and boulder deposits.<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><bold>Seeding</bold>: <computeroutput>seed_rocks_talus</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>seed_rocks_distribution</computeroutput> generate rocks following Pareto power-law distributions, where small rocks outnumber large boulders according to <computeroutput>power_law_alpha</computeroutput>.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><bold>Simulation</bold>: Geomorphological sorting via <computeroutput>simulate_rock_sorting</computeroutput> sorts items downslope according to gravitational energy and substrate friction, adjusting spatial distribution based on terrain gradient.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>
<sect1 id="md_docs_2content_2dev_2scatter__field_1autotoc_md49">
<title>4. Converting to Raster Fields</title>
<para>A <computeroutput>ScatterField</computeroutput> (and by extension <computeroutput>Forest</computeroutput> or <computeroutput>RockField</computeroutput>) can be rasterized back into the elevation domain:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><computeroutput>to_density_map</computeroutput>: Generates a continuous <computeroutput>Array</computeroutput> indicating entity density per cell.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para><computeroutput>to_heightmap</computeroutput>: Splats discrete geometric profiles (disks, cones, pyramids, smooth domes) onto an existing <computeroutput>Array</computeroutput> using maximum or additive blending. </para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
</sect1>
